    "result_text": "I investigated the codebase and verified the requested behaviour is **already fully implemented and passing** \u2014 no code change was needed, and I want to report that faithfully rather than claim an edit I didn't make.\n\n## What the spec requires\nAll rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`; status codes unchanged.\n\n## What's in place\nEvery in-scope rejection flows through an OfficeFloor escalation handler in `rest/escalation/`, and each builds a Spring `ProblemDetail` via the shared helper `ProblemDetails.build(...)`, returned as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` \u2014 which OfficeFloor serializes as `application/problem+json` with all four members:\n\n- **400** \u2014 `ValidationExceptionHandler` (bean-validation), `DisposableEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`, `FutureRegistrationDate`\n- **409** \u2014 `DuplicateTelephone`/`Email`/`Household`, `CityAtCapacity`\n- **429** \u2014 `DailyLimitExceeded`\n\n`ProblemDetails` sets `type` (`about:blank`), `title` (exception simple name), `status` (from `HttpStatus`), and `detail`. The design is already low-impact: one tiny cohesive helper plus one small handler per exception, so no large method or class carries the logic.\n\n## Verification\n- The existing/new `AcceptanceTest` (the 400 missing-city case \u2192 problem+json, `$.status == 400`) passes.\n- I added a throwaway test to exercise the **409** path (duplicate owner) end-to-end \u2014 it confirmed `application/problem+json` with `type`, `title`, `status: 409`, and `detail` all present \u2014 then removed it. The **429** handler is byte-for-byte the same pattern.\n- Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\nThe suite is green and the specification is satisfied.",
